Subject: [PATCH 2/7] feat(floor-studio): read the room, and say out loud what
 a photo cannot tell us
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FS-02 of the Floor Studio series.

lib/floor-studio/room.ts turns one uploaded photograph into five statistics
and four draggable corners. It is the screen the whole feature stands on,
because everything downstream — which floors get recommended, how the
render is lit, where the wood is laid — reads from it.

WHAT IT MEASURES, AND WHAT IT REFUSES TO

Measured, all of it arithmetic over pixels anybody can recompute: mean
relative luminance (WCAG 2.x, the same formula the contrast work uses),
the undertone of the upper third, the tone of the existing floor, and a
floor-plane quad found by sampling a reference colour at the bottom centre
and walking upward until the run of that colour collapses.

Refused: the square footage. A single uncalibrated photograph with nothing
of known size in it cannot yield an area, and every product in this
category that prints "approximately 320 sq ft" under a photo is guessing
and hoping nobody checks. The installed range is computed FROM the area,
so a quietly wrong area is a quietly wrong price — which is the one
category of error this business cannot absorb. So the area is ASKED FOR,
on the verification screen, with the reason printed next to the field.
Room type is the same: unmeasurable, consequential, therefore a question.
UNMEASURABLE_FROM_A_PHOTO exports both asks so every surface says it the
same way.

CONFIDENCE IS TWO WORDS, NOT A PERCENTAGE

floorConfidence is 'measured' or 'weak'. There is deliberately no third
confident-sounding value and no number: "87% confident" is a claim about a
distribution nobody estimated. When the run never collapses — a photo of a
floor rather than of a room — it returns the default quad, says weak, and
the notes tell the visitor to drag the corners. A test asserts no note
ever contains a confidence percentage.

NOTHING IS UPLOADED

PIPEDA case summary #2006-349 treats interior photographs as personal
information about the person who lives there; it is why
lib/floor-graph/photo-storage.ts refuses a public blob. The cheapest way
to honour that is not to collect the image. Every function here is pure
and takes { data, width, height } in RGBA — exactly what getImageData()
returns and exactly what a test can synthesise. No canvas, no DOM, no
network.

20 tests, asserted against synthetic rooms whose answer is known by
construction, with per-pixel jitter so the estimator is proven against a
surface rather than a paint chip.

